@0    = ~I must admit I'm impressed, Aerie. Rarely have I met one who could master both the arcane and divine arts.~
@1    = ~That's... that's very kind of you to say, Will. It took me a long time to learn how to wield my magic. I thought I would need many more years to master it, but Uncle Quayle said that I was ready.~
@2    = ~This Quayle... he's wasn't really your Uncle, correct?~
@3    = ~This Quayle... he wasn't really your Uncle, correct?~
@4    = ~Not by blood, no. But I am only alive thanks to him... he was the greatest man I have ever known... one of a kind..~
@5    = ~Not by blood, no. But I am alive only thanks to him... he's the greatest man I have ever known... one of a kind.~
@6    = ~Um... what about you, Will? Do you... do you miss your own parents?~
@7    = ~Sometimes, yes. It's been many years... no, centuries, since I last saw any of my relatives.~
@8    = ~I'm sorry it's been so long, Will. I... I'm not sure what else I can say.~
@9    = ~There's nothing to say, Aerie. I've accepted my current state of my existance. There's no changing what I am.~
@10   = ~Even so, it must still been hard for you... remaining as you are while the world changes.~
@11   = ~My life... my existence is no more or less difficult than anyone else's, Aerie. We all struggle, to some extent.~
@12   = ~I suppose you're right about that... but we do what we can to help others. <CHARNAME> has changed so many lives for the better.~
@13   = ~Indeed, you are correct. <PRO_HESHE> certainly has.~
@14   = ~You don't treat me as I would've expected from a priest, Aerie. Most would have tried to end my existence on sight, but you - you chose to accept me. At least... I feel as though you have.~
@15   = ~I... I don't think I should be afraid of you, Will. Should I be?~
@16   = ~Heh, no. Of course not.~
@17   = ~Uncle Quayle told me many tales, while I was still learning how to heal wounds. I remember him teaching me that one should never judge anyone on sight. No matter how... peculiar they might be.~
@18   = ~I believe we all have our peculiarities. Some are simply not as transparent as others.~
@19   = ~Yes... you may be right about that. Perhaps... perhaps others would condemn you, but I don't want to. <CHARNAME> trusts you, and I trust <PRO_HIMHER>... and you haven't done anything to betray us.~
@20   = ~I have never intended to betray anyone, Aerie. I joined <CHARNAME> out of gratitude for <PRO_HISHER> restoration of Visionhive. I trust <PRO_HIMHER> just as you do, and I trust you as well.~
@21   = ~I'm... truly glad to hear that, Will. Thank you.~
@22   = ~That's hardly something you need to be thankful for.~
@23   = ~No, I- I think there is.~
@24   = ~Aerie... there is something I feel we must talk about.~
@25   = ~Yes, Will? What is it?~
@26   = ~It's about Quayle. You had a dream about him last night, correct?~
@27   = ~Y-yes...! How... how did you know?~
@28   = ~It's the sight. I saw it.~
@29   = ~I don't know enough to be sure, but... I think he is trying to tell you that you're doing great. You're a skilled adventurer and powerful mage, and you are strong enough to succeed.~
@30   = ~Are these words yours or... or his?~
@31   = ~They are your own, I believe. They are words he left you long time ago. Do not doubt them.~
@32   = ~I... I'll try.~
@33   = ~I have a feeling that you despise me, Anomen, despite the fact that I have done nothing to harm you. Why?~
@34   = ~Why, you ask? Because the undead can never be trusted, no matter who they were in life. Your current state is nothing but a pale shadow, shaman, an echo of what might have been a good man. That you exist, and the power you wield, trouble me greatly.~
@35   = ~My power is little different from yours, Anomen. After all, we both use them for similar ends, don't we?~
@36   = ~Perhaps, but an unholy god may grant his followers power just as Helm does for me. The fact that we may both heal does nothing to give me trust.~
@37   = ~So, all in all, you think I'm just a vicious spirit that wishes to haunt you, yes?~
@38   = ~Not in such vicious terms, but... if that is how you wish to interpret them, then do so.~
@39   = ~Do you think I find that amusing? That my own companion sees me as a ghoulish creature thirsting for blood?~
@40   = ~Your existence is anything but amusing, ghost.~
@41   = ~...I will say it again, Anomen: I did not try to offend you.~
@42   = ~Perhaps, but my family matters are no business of yours, ghost. If there is any humanity left in you, then you will respect my privacy or never try to speak with me again.~
@43   = ~Your misplaced anger will lead you nowhere, Anomen. Is it all because I am a spectre, or is there more to it?~
@44   = ~You are an undead that speaks with others of your kind, leeching your energy from no righteous source. What other reason must I have to despise you? You should consider yourself fortunate that I tolerate your presence at all.~
@45   = ~Not all spirits are evil, Anomen. Can't you accept that?~
@46   = ~I only accept <CHARNAME>'s judgment that we must co-operate, and nothing more than that. Only <PRO_HISHER> command keeps you safe, nothing more. Away, ghost! I do not wish to speak with you again.~
@47   = ~I am impressed with your knowledge of herbs, Cernd. I have some knowledge of herblore myself, but you seem to know exactly where to seek them, as if the plants themselves tell you where to search.~
@48   = ~Why, thank you, Will. I have studied herblore for many years. One must aways observe their surroundings, for all of Nature's children - from the leaves to the roots - live in symbiosis. Each cultivates a particular type of plant.~
@49   = ~Even though I observe them the same way you appear to, you still seem to understand more than anyone else.~
@50   = ~I believe such things come with experience. I may have some skill in wielding primal energies, for example, but you are my senior in such skills. You commune with spirits in ways I have never witnessed, while my knowledge is of living things, such as plants and their influence on the body.~
@51   = ~Perhaps there is sense in that. Rarely am I required use herbs save for certain rituals. After all, the injury I suffer asks not for herbs, but for raw energy itself to heal.~
@52   = ~All things develop their own unique shapes and meanings to thrive in this world, Will, and of course, our knowledge will differ as a result. We bear scars of different origins and see different world. The only truth there is lies between our purposes.~
@53   = ~You would have made a fine shaman, Cernd. You have a great knowledge and a keen mind unlike any other I've known.~
@54   = ~Perhaps I may have, yet this is the path I saw fit to follow. Come now, Will. I believe we shouldn't slow <CHARNAME> down. There is yet much to do.~
@55   = ~While I still lived... after I left home, I admit there were times in which I missed city life. My father was a merchant, and so long as I was home I never needed to worry if I could find a book which interested me, or clothes that suited me.~
@56   = ~Civilization does tend to create dependencies in men used to it. I had similar feelings after I left Athkatla to study among the druids of the Tethyrian grove. I realized that I had grown used to the things I had taken for granted.~
@57   = ~You seem to have forgotten about them rather quickly, however.~
@58   = ~Even if a mighty oak is felled, the roots and moss it carries are taken with it, Will. In truth, there are certain habits I had never truly broken.~
@59   = ~Such as?~
@60   = ~Is this something you are truly wish to talk about, Will?~
@61   = ~You said it yourself, Cernd. These are our own roots.~
@62   = ~Very well... I admit I still have a taste for man-made confections, such as the mint-favored chocolates my wife used to buy once every month. We would always save at least a small piece for the next day, so that the taste would last.~
@63   = ~But those days are long behind me, Will. Come, as there are many things in the present that require our attention.~
@64   = ~I have heard things of your kind, Edwin.~
@65   = ~You claim to know of superior men, spectre? Those that deserve to rule, but are instead oft bothered by imbeciles and worthless baboons?~
@66   = ~Not that. I have heard of the Red Wizards of Thay, however. I know you and your arcane brothers are but monsters.~
@67   = ~Ha! Do not presume to understand the Red Wizards, foolish gibbon. After all, you are a mere mistake, a ghost too pitiful to leave this world behind. Even the bones of the dead, raised automatons, could be considered superior by virtue of having purpose! (Not to mention *they* would know their place, yes...)~
@68   = ~Don't make the mistake of underestimating me, Edwin. That may haunt you for your entire life.~
@69   = ~The only thing I find haunting is your lack of potency within this meager band (In more ways than one, I might add.)~
@70   = ~If you think your attempts at harming with words affect me, Edwin, then you're mistaken. I will only remind you that spirits are not to be underestimated. They know much of your kind, more than you know, Red Wizard.~
@71   = ~I advise you in this: do not provoke fate. You risk facing a much quieter end than my own. Nothing awaits you... except silence.~
@72   = ~...I will not continue this discussion any more, Edwin. I am no creature, summoned and bound by your magic. I have no cause or desire to serve you.~
@73   = ~Bah! At least those that are summoned possess the wisdom to follow my commands. You, by comparison, are a worthless, dead thing. Only the primal energies you wield are of interest me. Tell me all that you know of your magic... if you even understand it at all, that is.~
@74   = ~I will tell you nothing. If you were meant to understand such powers, they would already be under your control. Spirits decides who deserve their aid, and you must have been found wanting.~
@75   = ~Do you think I don't know what you're after? I know everything your kind has done. Your mages slaughtered countless spirits in Rashemen, defiled their sacred land. I will never help one such as you, as you will never deserve the knowledge you seek.~
@76   = ~Damn you, you imbicile! Rrrgh... very well, do as you wish gibbon. You may yet have some use, but right now, you are no better than the pile of ash you should be. Get out of my sight!~
@77   = ~It would be my pleasure, wizard.~
@78   = ~The longer I bear your presence, Edwin, the more my patience wanes. I will never understand why <CHARNAME> allows you to remain with us. You are nothing but a parasite.~
@79   = ~I remain because <CHARNAME> recognizes my far superior potential to your own, ghost. (If I had the power and knowledge to erase this thing once and for all, I wouldn't hesitate a single second to do so.)~
@80   = ~I'm sorry for asking this, but...~
@81   = ~Well now! You have observed me for many an hour, my... ghastly companion, before finally mustering the courage to speak. I do not believe I have ever been given such looks, nay, not once!~
@82   = ~Again, I'm sorry. I didn't mean to make you feel uncomfortable. I have nothing against your kind, Haer'Dalis. I am only curious.~
@83   = ~Uncomfortable, you say? Nay, that is not the feeling I bear. I know all too well the peculiarity that my kind is to the Prime, and I find consider it a compliment. In this world, I am a rare bird, one which some would consider an unknown predator.~
@84   = ~It must be the aura you possess. I feel it not, but I see it. I suppose it must be something in your blood... or perhaps it is the way your spirit sings. It causes feelings of uncertainty within others.~
@85   = ~My spirit is capable of singing many tunes, but aye, I understand your thoughts clearly, Will. I am aware of the tension that my aura brings to these Primes. Though, sadly, what might I do but endure?~
@86   = ~If it is of any comfort to you, then you should know that it does not affect all around you... myself, one not of this plane, for example. Your aura is beautiful, as I see it.~
@87   = ~That may be true. I know there are exceptional ones with whom my presence does not inspire fear, but... fascination.~
@88   = ~I... I think it's a matter of finding inspiration in someone, rather than fear...~
@89   = ~True. You have my gratitude, my fair dove, for the courage to bring forth such beautiful words. Well, as you can see, Will, you are not alone in your thoughts.~
@90   = ~I can see that.~
@91   = ~I still have questions to ask, but perhaps I should save them. I'm sure we'll have plenty of time to talk without slowing down the group.~
@92   = ~More questions, aye? I shall await them, then, but for now let's chance our fates and continue our march.~
@93   = ~Who are the Doomguard, Haer'Dalis?~
@94   = ~A curious question, my spectral friend. I assume you are unfamiliar with Sigil?~
@95   = ~Not at all. I'm not sure I even know where that is. Is it somewhere close to Sembia?~
@96   = ~Ha! Nay, not close, and perhaps that is fortunate for the Primes. Sigil... the City of Doors, is not of this world. It is home to endless gates and pathways, each of which may lead you to wonders or doom. A perfect place for those who like to put their lives to the test.~
@97   = ~Maybe it is. So the Doomguards live there? Are they all from Sigil?~
@98   = ~Some, aye, but not all.~
@99   = ~Our lot - myself as but one - believe that fate leads us to oblivion, and that there is beauty in the inevitable. The multiverse is fated to meet its end within Entropy and Chaos.~
@100  = ~Entropy? Chaos? Aren't those the names of your swords?~
@101  = ~Aye, I see your mind is as sharp as these steel fangs I bear, Will. Indeed - Entropy and Chaos, my trusted companions. Can you say there is a better way to admire such things than to wield them in a grand dance in battle?~
@102  = ~Hmm, I'm afraid nothing comes to mind. Come, Haer'Dalis! Let's move on.~
@103  = ~Yes, Haer'Dalis? Is there something you need?~
@104  = ~Ah, no, my friend. Carry on, I was merely musing on whether to brand you a nighthawk or a kakapo.~
@105  = ~A 'kakapo'...?~
@106  = ~The kakapo is a nocturnal bird, green and flightless. They closely resemble owls and parrots. I thought the comparison with you to be apt, my friend. Nocturnal, yet colorful, and bound to the earth.~
@107  = ~I see. Strange as it seems, it makes sense.~
@108  = ~How does it feel, my fair shaman, to be bound to this plane even after the death of your body, now buried in cold soil and held in the silent earth's chilly embrace?~
@109  = ~Everything feels different. I'm afraid there's no other way to describe it to the living. Things sometimes appear faded, worn, but there are other things which I see more clearly than ever. Spirits, auras.~
@110  = ~So you have hte power to behind that which is unseen to the living? That is a gift, my friend, a gift that many would kill for! My dearest Will, I am certain that the members of my former troupe, miss Raelis especially, would view such a gift as the greatest treasure, for imagine all the tales she could make of them upon the grand stage!~
@111  = ~Hopefully she won't need such a gift for quite a while. Come, we should be moving.~
@112  = ~Are you feeling well, Imoen?~
@113  = ~Yes... I think so, thank you. Why do you ask?~
@114  = ~There's something strange about you. You seem... hollow. As I recall, you have had trouble sleeping. I have seen the signs... I mean, it's a habit. I don't sleep myself, so I take the whole watch.~
@115  = ~I... I don't mind. You're right, I've been getting strange feelings when I sleep. I'm not sure if I can explain it, but if you know how it feels when you close your eyes, then think you've heard something, so you open your eyes... but imagine that you can't see anything, and you must keep your eyes closed even though you think something's happening and that...~
@116  = ~...that someone may come, unseen, and do things to you, tell things, even hurt you.~
@117  = ~You've been through hell and survived, Imoen. No matter how you may feel now, you are strong, just like <CHARNAME>.~
@118  = ~Thanks, Will. I think I needed that. Come, I think we should get moving.~
@119  = ~Do you have a moment, Imoen?~
@120  = ~Yeah, sure. You need something?~
@121  = ~No. Actually, I'm the one who has something for you.~
@122  = ~If you experience any problems with sleeping again, these herbs can make a brew when boiled. They may help you... at least a little bit.~
@123  = ~Thanks, Will. That's really nice of you. I... I may indeed use them.~
@124  = ~Let me know if you need more.~
@125  = ~I will.~
@126  = ~I know it's probably a silly question, but... isn't that strange that you're called Will and you can summon will-o'-wisps? I mean... Isn't that like having a woman called Rose who sells roses?~
@127  = ~It may be a coincidence, or it may not be.~
@128  = ~I'm not sure if I understand what you mean, Will.~
@129  = ~Perhaps everything happens because of the simplest coincidences. I once had a nickname - "will-o'-wisps" - because I loved ghost stories and fairy tales as a kid. That may have developed into something more.~
@130  = ~I actually liked being called that nickname, and maybe that is why I decided to continue this fondness. Perhaps that's why I followed the path even further when I realized that I can see and hear more than others.~
@131  = ~You are a powerful woman, Jaheira.~
@132  = ~Thank you, Will. My skills were born of necessity. Gorion once told me that his child may need my assistance, and I saw fit to prepare.~
@133  = ~You mean <CHARNAME>? I can hardly think of <PRO_HIMHER> as a child. <PRO_HESHE> is a powerful <PRO_MANWOMAN>.~
@134  = ~Indeed, <PRO_HESHE> may be now, but <PRO_HESHE> was but a child not long ago, away from the safety of Candlekeep for the first time. Though I admit your words are true... <PRO_HESHE> is far stronger now compared to the child I once knew.~
@135  = ~I think I can say that you must have changed, too.~
@136  = ~I may have, but I have not thought much on that. Too much has occurred in this adventure, first with Sarevok and the Iron Throne, then with the crusade of the Shining Lady. Even now, we pursue the madman Irenicus. Yes... much time was lost, but it was given to a good cause.~
@137  = ~You're right, but I can't help but think there's something else you do not wish to speak of.~
@138  = ~That may be the case, but a good man would respect my decision to keep quiet. We should move on, Will.~
@139  = ~I have a question for you, Jaheira.~
@140  = ~I am listening, Will. What is it?~
@141  = ~Who was Khalid?~
@142  = ~Wh-where did you hear that name? I have not spoken it to you, not as I recall. Speak!~
@143  = ~Relax. You did not tell me. Spirits whispered to me when we last spoke.~
@144  = ~Ah, spirits. It is always the spirits with you. If they listen, tell them to mind their own business!~
@145  = ~I think we both know it doesn't quite work that way, Jaheira.~
@146  = ~*sigh* Yes, I... I know. I should not blame you. But some memories should... remain in one's heart. I remember too much of Khalid... my dear husband... the ways he would look at me, speak to me... those memories are still too fresh. I would keep them safe from others. It is not painful, but I owe Khalid that much.~
@147  = ~I didn't know, Jaheira. Sometimes... all I hear are brief words and broken sentences. That's how it was this time.~
@148  = ~Very well. I believe you, shaman. But I would rather you ask me no more questions, and leave me to my thoughts. I require my own space.~
@149  = ~As you wish, Jaheira.~
@150  = ~Hmm, listen, Will lad, looking at you has brought a memory to ol' Jan's noggin... once upon a time, I saw a most unusual painting at my cousin Jeklem's, just hanging on his wall. Now what was it called... ah, yes! "The Portrait of Count Camaclas", that's it! Oh, and a fine portrait it was. I may be no connoiseur of art, but I know a fine picture when I see one. As I recall, Jeklam told me he spent a small fortune acquiring it, give or take an arm or a leg. Er... figuratively speaking, of course. Did you know you are almost the spitting image of Count Camaclas? Well, he had a wider jaw, for certain, but the resemblance is still uncanny!~
@151  = ~I'm not sure what you want me to say to that, Jan. I've so much as heard of this man, so I can't possibly be him. I doubt we shared any blood either.~
@152  = ~Is that so, lad? Are you certain he wasn't an unknown relative? Perhaps an uncle, or grand-uncle or what have you? Perhaps the name was unwelcome among your family from the man boasting of his fortunes from smuggling old, festering vegetables and selling them to local taverns for a profit? Er, I am certain no self-respecting Jansen would ever do such a thing, at least not openly, but the possibilities, ah... now, where were we? Oh, yes, Count Camaclas, wasn't it? I still think you look just like him, hence why I wondered if he might have been a relative, but... I'm sure you know your family better than I do.~
@153  = ~I'm fairly sure I do. Why would I hide anything about my relatives from you?~
@154  = ~Why, there could always be a reason! Perhaps it was the aformentioned rotten (heh, rotten...) trade of merchandise that I spoke of that squandered his reputation, and you do not with to be associated with the man's name? Such things happen in the vegetable trade, as I know too well, I'm afraid. Not very often, of course, as the bad eggs are removed quickly, but certainly not unheard of.~
@155  = ~I'll take your word on it, but I meant it when I said I've never heard the man's name.~
@156  = ~And I, um... don't think you should see your cousin too often. I have a feeling he gets on your nerves.~
@157  = ~Good cousin Jeklem? Heavens, no! Never. Well... perhaps sometimes. Or it's quite often. Ever since that rotten vegetables incident...~
@158  = ~I thought so. Well, in any case... let's both take a breath and... let's move, shall we? We shouldn't keep <CHARNAME> waiting.~
@159  = ~I'm telling you, Will, even in your current state, you could surely appreciate the power of a good healthy turnip or something similar. Well, they may not be able to bolster your body's strength anymore, but-~
@160  = ~*sigh* I'm not sure how you think it'll help a spectre, Jan. How do you even expect me to eat it?~
@161  = ~Well, you certainly would not be able to reap the benefits personally, of course, I can't deny that, but surely you have relatives among the living. Oh, sure, we went over that bit of misconception with Count Camaclas, but surely you must know others of your bloodline. I'm sure you could persuade them into seeing the benefits of a three-course turnip diet! A healthy and long life in the making!~
@162  = ~I think you're just missing your line of work, Jan. Far too much, for that matter.~
@163  = ~How do you feel fighting with an undead being on your side, knight? I know your kind would rather fight against spectral creatures of my kind rather than alongside them, if possible.~
@164  = ~Aye, that is true of those of my calling, spirit. But we also recognize the presence of evil in others, and judge all things fairly and accordingly where we are able. So long as you do not cause harm to our allies, nor the innocent, I see no reason to oppose you.~
@165  = ~Do you not fear the possibility of being branded as a traitor to your code?~
@166  = ~Nay, Will. The Order of the Radiant Heart lives for the goal of vanquishing evil. You, however, are not threatened by the ideals that I follow, are you?~
@167  = ~No, I'm not.~
@168  = ~Then what cause have I to destroy you?~
@169  = ~You've got me here.~
@170  = ~Hah! Then you understand me well. Will. I may be old, but I am no fanatic yet. You have little to fear.~
@171  = ~I were thinking about our last talk, Keldorn.~
@172  = ~I expected as much. Perhaps there are still questions or doubts that plague your mind?~
@173  = ~Not exactly. I was thinking of apologizing, actually.~
@174  = ~What have you to apologize for, Will? Have you done harm to anyone undeserving of it?~
@175  = ~No. I was just wrong for feeling insecure when <CHARNAME> introduced us. I believed all paladins were the same and-~
@176  = ~You were mistaken and learned from the experience. That is all I need know, Will. No harm was done, for you acted as you should have. So long as you do not see me as a potential enemy, I am satisfied.~
@177  = ~Hey! Make use of yer mojo-tricks here, ghost-boy. I be havin' a case of the aches that needs solvin', hear?~
@178  = ~What is it, Korgan? I can barely understand you, but if you are bruised again, perhaps this ointment may help.~
@179  = ~Good! Rub it here, har har!~
@180  = ~What--?! Do you find this amusing? I'd rather you put that thing where it belongs... anywhere but there.~
@181  = ~A-har-har! Ye shoulda seen yer face, spirit-boy! Reckon ye never seen one half as large, have ye? Now ye know me secret ter swingin' me axe, eh? I have me experience at swingin', har!~
@182  = ~You're full of surprised, Korgan...~
@183  = ~Ha! Ye ain't seen nothin' yet, boy. This be a surprise for all that seen it!~
@184  = ~Ugh, I meant- forget it. I don't even want to say anything about that...~
@185  = ~Yer paler than a ghost, boy. Been dreamin' 'bout that thing that I showed ye, eh? Havin' nightmares? Har!~
@186  = ~No. I do not sleep, ergo, I do not dream.~
@187  = ~A shame, that is. Ye can't be seein' the blood and gold I swim in then. Mebbe ye'd settle fer another glimpse, eh?~
@188  = ~Why not? Show me what you've got.~
@189  = ~...~
@190  = ~Ha! Har har! Not bad, boy. Ye've got a pair of balls after all!~
@191  = ~If you find your pleasure in exhibiting your 'weapon' to a dead man, then go ahead. I know better than to judge another's fetish. Heh.~
@192  = ~Oh, ye got me good there, ghost! Ha, ye really does have balls after all. Clear ones, fer sure, but they're there!~
@193  = ~So, 'twas some time since someone last fondled yer staff, eh, ghostie? Guess ye didn't die a virgin at least?~
@194  = ~No. I was thirty-three at the time of my death. Far from a virgin, if you must know. Do you have a reason for bringing this up, or do you simply enjoy talking about intimate matters?~
@195  = ~Har! I see no reason it cannae be both, eh? Yer not so bad for a man rottin' beneath some grave or other. I'm sure ye had yer share of fun before... becoming a stiff fer life.~
@196  = ~I had my time. It was with another man. He... died, however, years before I met the same fate. It wasn't the same with others, but I laid with them, too. Not as many as I'm sure you have, but I was far from celibate.~
@197  = ~Heh heh! Good ter see ye had the sense ter enjoy life while ye still had it, lad. Every fool should know the pleasure of a wild ride before they die.~
@198  = ~So, ye've slept with other men, eh, boy? I be nae an expert in such matters, but I cannae see it bein' anythin' but sticky, har!~
@199  = ~I can't say I know myself, but don't such thing always get... as you said... sticky?~
@200  = ~Aye, true enough, but double the men means double the mess, eh?~
@201  = ~Not sure what I can even say to that.~
@202  = ~Then I'll be assumin' me assumptions were right. Now, let's be findin' some fool to cut down. Me axe is thirstin' for a bloodbath!~
@203  = ~I have watched you in battle, Mazzy. You have a certain pattern in your style, as if you know when your opponent will strike and when to retaliate. It is fascinating.~
@204  = ~You may consider it Arvoreen's blessing, or my own skill. There is little difference. I observe every foe I face down, Will. I do not fight to murder, but to defend myself, others, and anything else that requires my protection.~
@205  = ~But I am curious... what is it about me that you find fascinating? I feel I should know.~
@206  = ~I have my reasons for it, but you may find them... unusual.~
@207  = ~Do not think my words are meant to offend you, Will, but I doubt it can be anything more unusual than our current partnership.~
@208  = ~That's partially the reason. I've never met a warrior like you before. A halfling who fights like a brawler.~
@209  = ~Excuse me? I try to retain some finesse and tact in my style. I would hardly consider myself a "brawler".~
@210  = ~I'm sorry. That was probably the wrong term to use. A warrior, then. A halfling warrior, and earnest adventurer.~
@211  = ~Fair enough. I find that more to my liking. As for why you have never seen another of my kind, well - perhaps you have not searched well enough. There are many who follow the same calling as I do. Perhaps they choose to fight differently, but we share the same valiant hearts, I assure you.~
@212  = ~Who is this 'Arvoreen' that you worship, Mazzy?~
@213  = ~Arvoreen, the Vigilant Guardian, is the protector of my people, Will. She is otherwise known as the Defender and the Wary Sword to her followers. Why does this interest you?~
@214  = ~No reason in particular. I just wanted to point out how devoted you appear to be.~
@215  = ~That is no strange thing to true believers of a certain faith, Will. Do you have your own target of devotion?~
@216  = ~Not to a name. I follow the primal energies that live within the land, and sometimes, within other people. Besides that, I worship the beauty of strange phenomena, such as the stars, the moons, or even the whispers.~
@217  = ~The whispers?~
@218  = ~Yes. Quiet, hushed whispers, though easily stifled by a louder voice, can also have great influence, and change the tides of many battles. In other times, they can forge lasting memories for those who know to find them.~
@219  = ~Often I find your words more mysterious than any book I've read, shaman.~
@220  = ~Is it true, Minsc? You're from the land of Rashemen? That land truly exists?~
@221  = ~Yes, it is as real as Minsc and Boo, my ghost friend! Rashemen is far away, but it is close to our hearts, right, Boo? Ah, Minsc still has fond memories of the lodge, as well as the city of Mulsantir, the Lake Tirulag, and many other places Minsc cannot say the names of. We have travelled far, and kicked many butts along the way.~
@222  = ~Unfortunately I've never seen any of those places with my own eyes, Minsc. But if it does exist, then it must be a fantastic place. I've heard many things about it.~
@223  = ~It is, my friend, for otherwise neither Minsc nor Boo could proud to call it home! Well, Boo has never seen it either, but he knows Minsc speaks truth in his heart of hearts! *squeak*~
@224  = ~Do you miss that land? Your home?~
@225  = ~We dream of it every night, my friend. Boo has a wilder imagination, I think, for he only has that to see great Rashemen with. But we could never return with raised chests until the evil Irenicus is kicked in his dirty, murderous butt!~
@226  = ~I see. Well, I think Boo should be proud to have such a valiant companion like yourself.~
@227  = ~Come, we should be going. We're falling behind.~
@228  = ~So, for you, all of this started as a... 'dajemma' did you call it?~
@229  = ~Yes, that is correct! Though there were meant to be three: Minsc, of course, along with Boo, and... our witch. Dynaheir.~
@230  = ~Gah, I see the lout is still hung up over that damned wench. (A pity I could not claim the honor of taking her life for myself.)~
@231  = ~Speak not of her name, Red Wizard, lest you feel the mighty wrath of Minsc!~
@232  = ~Dynaheir was my hathran, friend Will, and the charge of my protection. Minsc's life was meant for her safety, yet... Minsc failed, and she has fallen.~
@233  = ~I see. Was it... Irenicus?~
@234  = ~Shh, friend! Boo dislikes hearing Dynaheir's name, for he still misses our witch greatly. (quietly) Yes, Irenicus, the evil wizards, took her from us. Another reason for vengeance, so that he will not harm another innocent!~
@235  = ~I understand, Minsc. If there's anything you ever need to say about it, then I'll be here to listen.~
@236  = ~You should take care of that scratch, Nalia.~
@237  = ~Hmm? What... oh, yes, you're right, Will. I'm sorry, it's just been so hard to focus. I've had a hard time sleeping with all the bad dreams lately.~
@238  = ~I assume it's got something to do with your family's land.~
@239  = ~Yes, that's precisely it. I just can't stop thinking about it.~
@240  = ~I can't help you with your troubles unless <CHARNAME> has any ideas, but about the dreams... I could prepare something that might help. A sleeping drought, or something similar.~
@241  = ~Thank you for offering, Will, but I don't think that will be necessary. My thoughts are about silly things that I can't change now. You should save your expertise for something more important than my nightmares, like those in serious need.~
@242  = ~Nightmares can be no less harmful than any disease, Nalia. They can weaken you if you cling to them, and cause a certain decay to begin. I would not underestimate your problems so quickly if I were you.~
@243  = ~You've been mumbling in your sleep, Nalia.~
@244  = ~Have I? I hope I didn't disturb anyone from their rest.~
@245  = ~No, but it would mean that your nightmares still haunt you. Am I wrong?~
@246  = ~I still get them sometimes, but is that really unusual? Everyone who dreams have nightmares. Please don't take it personally, but I won't need any medicine for this. I'll be fine. I'm sure of it.~
@247  = ~Alright. I don't want to force you if it's bothersome.~
@248  = ~No, not in the slightest. I'm glad you care, Will, it's just that... I want to fight it out on my own. I need to be strong enough for that, at least.~
@249  = ~Fine. But remember: even if you would rather help others instead of yourself, sometimes the most important thing is to take care of yourself when you need to.~
@250  = ~I've seen you once or twice within dreams, ranger, while I was still a part of the Visionhive.~
@251  = ~That's not too much of a surprise. My cabin isn't far from that grotto, after all. I must have been near it a few times.~
@252  = ~But did you ever hear about the Visionhive's power? I wonder if anyone alive knew about it?~
@253  = ~Hmm. While I was a child, my father told me a tale once, about a cave in which a spirit dwelled, whispering words within the ears of those that entered its home.~
@254  = ~That may be a tale of the Visionhive in a shallow sense. Perhaps what you heard was once used as a campfire tale to spook or thrill its listeners.~
@255  = ~Perhaps. Or it may have simply been one of your fellow spirits with a very bad sense of humor. Regardless, this talk serves no purpose, so let's move on.~
@256  = ~Valygar, your dreams last night left small fragments that revealed themselves to me. I could see... magic in those dreams.~
@257  = ~My dreams are my own business, shaman. You may have the gift of sight, but that gives you no right to peer into our thoughts, whether they be waking or resting. If you cannot put a leash on your curiosity, then keep your distance. And I would thank you to keep what you have learned to yourself.~
@258  = ~I can see you value your privacy. Fine. I won't say a word to anyone else.~
@259  = ~Why must you stare at me, athiyk? Do the flames of passion light what passes for blood in your veins? Do you lust for the feel of my skin? Ha! You will never know the feel, shaman, so look if you will. It amuses me to think you will never know more.~
@260  = ~I have no desire for one such as you, Viconia. I don't find you attractive in any way.~
@261  = ~You are either a poor liar or a fool, athiyk. What do you find attractive, then?~
@262  = ~No drow woman, or woman of any kind. If you must know, I preferred men while I still lived.~
@263  = ~Hmph. That would explain why you seem immune to my appeal. I find it revolting, but I can hardly affect you, athiyk. Some males in drow society shared similar tastes, though they hid it well if they wished to live. I am not opposed to them, in fact... one such male assisted me in my escape from Menzoberranzan, though he paid dearly for it.~
@264  = ~You just admitted that someone helped you? Who are you and what you did to Viconia, doppelganger?.~
@265  = ~Make a further jest, shaman, and I assure you that neither I nor the Nightsinger will show you any mercy.~
@266  = ~Perhaps that was wrong. I didn't intend to mock. I just find it surprising that you would ever admit that someone - much less a man - helped you.~
@267  = ~Do you think that unique? You are replaceable, just as <CHARNAME> can be if stronger allies cross my path. I have received aid from many in the past, both male and female. I mentioned that drow male not out of respect for his actions, but to ensure you that anyone is expendable!~
@268  = ~If you say so.~
@269  = ~So, athiyk, indulge my curiosity - what is it about males that arouses you? Is it their strength? Their roughness? Or is it their foolishness, that allows them to be easily controlled?~
@270  = ~I've never wanted to control anyone before, Viconia, so you are mistaken. I do not believe there is anything in particular that attracts me. I simply enjoy male... features. It may be their beards or their musky smell, but what I valued the most is their character. They allow me to feel safe and happy.~
@271  = ~Bah. I would have accepted their scent as an excuse, but do you truly believe that others can make you feel safe? A dagger in hand would provide more safety than another thinking being. Anyone with a mind can plot against you.~
@272  = ~Does that extend to <CHARNAME>? Do you not feel safe in <PRO_HISHER> company either?~
@273  = ~That is a different matter altogether. You have no business with what my thoughts towards <CHARNAME> may be.~
@274  = ~I guess that means you'd sooner die than admit that someone else could ever give you safety.~
@275  = ~Where do you hail from, Yoshimo?~
@276  = ~I, my friend? I hail from faraway Kozakura, in the venerable land of Kara-Tur, my friend. It is an oriental place, full of beauty unlike anything in this land.~
@277  = ~The name rings a bell, slightly, but I cannot remember from when or why.~
@278  = ~We have customs that have reached distant lands, including here, my friend. Perhaps it is the sharp edges of our katanas, or our deadly fighting technique? Or might it be our exotic teas?~
@279  = ~It might've been the blades, but... wait, tea? You mean the green kind? I remember my father once receiving several orders to import them from foreign lands.~
@280  = ~That is very possible, Will, for we Kozakurans revere our tea culture. Drinking it is something of a ritual to us, for it brings us warmth of body and peace of mind. Our tea leaves and spices are oft worth hundreds of gold for a mere handful to the right people.~
@281  = ~It's a pity I never had a chance to try it. Now, as I am, I... never will.~
@282  = ~That is truly a shame, my friend, but at least it is impossible to miss what you've never had. Myself, on the other hand, well... there are many things I would wish to come home to. Tea is but one of those things.~
@283  = ~I didn't realize your people believed in spirits, Yoshimo.~
@284  = ~Yes, my friend. We have many wise men skillful in speaking with the spirits of the land. Most known are followers of the Eight Million Gods, spirits of nature that bear a resemblance of ghosts. They often reside in lost rivers, mountains or groves that they protect, and impose rules upon their dwellers.~
@285  = ~I see. So those places are something like the Visionhive.~
@286  = ~Perhaps you might see it that way, but it is... less so. The spirits of Kara-Tur tend to act in... mysterious ways, unknown to the living. We of Kozakura rarely see any signs of their presence. No peculiarities, nor signs of spiritual energy.~
@287  = ~But didn't you also say there are those who can speak to those spirits? Can they not also see them, and what places are sacred?~
@288  = ~Well, most of the land has become sacred in our time, but I understand your meaning, my friend. Yes, there are such beings. The korobokuru - smallfolk who resemble dwarves or gnomes. Many of them follow your profession - the way of the shaman.~
@289  = ~This homeland of yours... it sounds very extraordinary, Yoshimo!~
@290  = ~Heh, that it may seem to you, Will, but we always have such feelings towards the unfamiliar. After all, here, I am the foreigner. Much of your culture is just as fascinating to me as ours is to you.~
@291  = ~I have heard many voices of spirits that despise you, Dorn. I know not what crimes you have committed upon them, but they must have been terrible.~
@292  = ~Terrible, you say, ghost? Ha! I consider my actions purposeful. I see even the ghosts of the afterlife know the name of Dorn Il-Khan is not to be underestimated. No doubt many of those that I have slaughtered are among those that whisper to you.~
@293  = ~The only thing terrible I see, ghost, is the lack of skill or potential in those who were delusional to think they could oppose me.~
@294  = ~I suppose you showed them no mercy before tearing them from this world.~
@295  = ~Mercy is a weakness, and weakness is the one thing I will never abide.~
@296  = ~How many men have tried to kill you in this life, Il-Khan?~
@297  = ~Countless, many of them fools. Many of them now lie in the dirt, their names cursed to be forgotten - a proper fate for the error of standing in my way. I can see from your condition that you failed to carve the same legacy. How did you die, ghost? No doubt in some pathetic and meaningless manner.~
@298  = ~It was a venom that ended my life.~
@299  = ~Venom? Ha! Even more pathetic than I imagined. One such as you should have had the knowledge to combat venoms. Had you the strength or knowledge, you would have survived, yet you did not.~
@300  = ~I lived as long as I could. Thirty-three years.~
@301  = ~Thirty-three years of meaningless life only to be slain by a crawling beast. How pitiful. You are not worthy of being under my gaze, spirit. Begone.~
@302  = ~Why are you looking at me, Neera? Is there something you need?~
@303  = ~Er, not looking at you, sorry. I was looking *through* you. It's kinda weird, you know, having a person you can see through in our group. I've never had someone I needed to squint at to recognize, even the ones I set on fire or, uh... turn into pinkish goo. Yeah, that's happened.~
@304  = ~But I hope you can at least get used to it...~
@305  = ~What, turning people into goo? Sure, I guess, it's happened twice so far. A few more times and I could change profession to a goo... er, 'goomanger'? 'Goovoker'? I dunno.~
@306  = ~I... didn't mean that, Neera. I meant me. Can you get used to travelling with a ghost? One that summons others of his kind?~
@307  = ~Um...~
@308  = ~Okay, I'll be honest. I got what you meant the first time. I just needed to distract you so I could think, y'know? Maybe avoid the question, because I'm not sure what to say yet. I'm not so used to it yet, but... maybe when we get to know each other. Stranger things have happened to me.~
@309  = ~Thank you. I don't want you to feel uncomfortable when I'm around.~
@310  = ~How are you feeling to<DAYNIGHT>, Neera? You look rather paler than usual.~
@311  = ~W-what? Oh, it's you. Nothing's wrong, just don't look at-~
@312  = ~By all the sacred spirits, what... what are those?~
@313  = ~Um... teeth, I guess.~
@314  = ~Why... why has your ration of cheese grown teeth?~
@315  = ~Erm... who knows? Maybe it's still young and hasn't lost its milk teeth? Eh-heheh...~
@316  = ~...You tried to grill it with a fire spell, didn't you?~
@317  = ~No! Er, maybe?~
@318  = ~I don't even know what to say, so... I'll just pretend this never happened.~
@319  = ~Right. Better for everyone involved. Meaning us.~
@320  = ~You have a surprising amount of herbal knowledge, Rasaad. Were you tutored extensively?~
@321  = ~A little, yes. There were many tomes in the monastery related to the subject. Whatever I could not learn from them, I taught myself through experience as I travelled.~
@322  = ~You must be a quick learner, then.~
@323  = ~Thank you, my friend, but I consider it simply borne out of necessity. I have no great talent.~
@324  = ~I doubt that. Your skills are very impressive, considering they're not even part of your training.~
@325  = ~There is still much I have yet to learn in this life. I hope this will not be a bother, but perhaps you would be willing to show me more. The knowledge could make a great difference one day, and I would learn before it is too late.~
@326  = ~With pleasure.~
@327  = ~These are Silverleaves, you see? They're relatively simple to use - simply crush their juice to make a drug capable of fighting lesser toxins.~
@328  = ~I see. That will be helpful. What of these roots? Are they not vital ingredients in curing food poisoning? I recall seeing them drawn in a book I once read.~
@329  = ~Yes, gravelickers. They are most often found growing in graveyards, close to old tombstones. That is the source of their common name. They grow so closely to the stones that their leaves would 'lick' them figuratively.~
@330  = ~The common name? I assume they were originally known as something else then?~
@331  = ~That's right. It's original name was... hmm, as I recall... ah, 'nuumaris voliurr', I believe.~
@332  = ~Fascinating. I have no doubt the knowledge will be of use as I travel to further lands, Will. You make for an inspiring teacher.~
@333  = ~I'm just repeating what I've read and memorized. Here, study this book for a while. You'll know about as much as I do once you're finished.~
@334  = ~Thank you, Will. I will return it as soon as I finish.~
@335  = ~Have you ever wondered how your life would have been if you weren't... what you are now?~
@336  = ~I would be dead, Will.~
@337  = ~You know what I mean.~
@338  = ~What good is there in thinking about the life I'll never have?~
@339  = ~Because it's a human thing to do. Wondering about... everything. Every possibility.~
@340  = ~I'm not human, Will. Not anymore.~
@341  = ~*sigh* Well, it looks like this discussion is going nowhere. It seems like it's lacking a... pulse...~
@342  = ~Is it true that you're from Chult?~
@343  = ~Yes, I was. Does it matter to you?~
@344  = ~I've heard stories of the land. I've heard it's a different world, made of jungles. Wild, bizarre, and inhabited by massive reptiles. Is it all real?~
@345  = ~Oh, yes. It doesn't stop at the dinosaurs. The climate, culture, even the gods are different to what you know. They are even more dangerous.~
@346  = ~I figured as much. You're not exactly the most peaceful or harmless being yourself, Hexxat.~
@347  = ~Good that you know.~
@348  = ~Your eyes are very calm, Wilson. I feel as though they accurately reflect all of your great skill and experience.~
@349  = ~Snuffle snort. Growl.~
@350  = ~Really? I didn't realize how long it took you to become accustomed with fighting humans and the like. You must have been through many battles with those firm claws of yours.~
@351  = ~Growl growl.~
@352  = ~Ah. I'm not surprised that had to change, then. I hope you're not bothered that I brought back those memories.~
@353  = ~Snort. Sniff.~
@354  = ~Good... I'm glad. Don't ever hesitate in asking me for help, friend. I would be honored to tend to you whenever you need.~
@355  = ~Really, Wilson? Ha, I never would have guessed such a thing, you old thug! Did you really take all their fish?~
@356  = ~Growl!~
@357  = ~Heh... you're right. They should have known that you'd return. After all, you caught it!~
@358  = ~Snufflesnuff.~
@359  = ~Good point. I guess they deserved it after all.~
@360  = ~Growl.~
@361  = ~Heh, I'm glad we're on the same side here, my friend.~
